WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.810
There probably hasn't been a bigger or more
dynamic year in recent television history

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:06.080
than 2018.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:11.600
Among all this great TV, certain moments proved
unforgettable in ways that, for better or

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.700
for worse, fans will be discussing for years.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:17.620
Here are the biggest TV moments of 2018, the
good and the bad.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.620
Henry Winkler has been a TV legend for more
than 40 years.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:26.939
In the 1970s, he was probably the most popular
cultural figure in America for his work on

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:33.180
Happy Days portraying leather-wearing, jukebox-smacking
ladies' man Arthur Fonzarelli, a.k.a. Fonzie.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:38.620
He received his first of six Emmy nominations
way back in 1976 for that role, but it took

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:43.830
until 2018 for Winkler, widely regarded as
one of the nicest guys in Hollywood, to finally

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.680
take home that prized statue for television
excellence.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:52.240
For his role as acting coach Gene Cosineau
on HBO's dark comedy Barry, Winkler won the

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.900
first trophy of the night at the 2018 Emmy
Awards and the audience leapt to their feet

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:00.980
as the former Fonzie delivered the speech
he'd written in 1976.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:01.880
The best part?

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.780
Telling his kids, who are full-grown adults,
that it was past their bedtimes.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.800
If you’ve been anywhere even close to the
Internet in the past few months, then you

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:15.009
know that prolific comic book writer and editor
Stan Lee, whose cameos in nearly every Marvel

00:01:15.009 --> 00:01:19.630
Movie made him a familiar figure to billions
of people across the world, passed away in

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:20.120
November.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.760
“I guess one person can make a difference.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:27.040
nuff said.”

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.340
With the news that the co-creator of the Marvel
Universe had died, accolades, praise, and

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.980
tributes came pouring in from people in all
walks of life.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:39.560
And then Real Time host Bill Maher weighed
in with a piece on his show’s blog called

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:45.220
"Adulting," a disrespectful dismissal of comics
in general, and by extension, Lee in particular.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.259
Claiming that Lee had only ever inspired his
fans to “go watch a movie, I guess,” Maher,

00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:54.729
who has never shied away from using snide
comments to get attention, advised comic book

00:01:54.729 --> 00:01:58.720
fans to grow up and move on to “big boy
books without the pictures.”

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:03.219
Presumably he means his books, which are all
about how smart Bill Maher is for knowing

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:04.200
about sarcasm.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.980
Maher dealt with the fallout of his remarks
on Hulu's Larry King Now.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.750
He didn't apologize, claiming that he wasn't
even aware he'd caused a hubbub, and clarified

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:16.840
that he'd meant that thinking comic books
and superhero movies could have artistic worth

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.060
was just the symptom of a stupid culture.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.260
Not a good look, especially given the circumstances.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:28.280
Donald Glover's first time hosting Saturday
Night Live in May 2018 was timed to promote

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:31.440
his role as young Lando in Solo: A Star Wars
Story.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:37.500
Unfortunately, between Atlanta, This Is America,
and absolutely killing it on NBC's late-night

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.480
institution, that movie might be the least
memorable thing Glover did all year.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.460
Glover shined in every single sketch he was
a part of.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:49.110
He played a sleazy lawyer defending Jurassic
World over the many violent dinosaur-related

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.050
deaths for which it was certainly liable.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:56.260
He did musical comedy in a high-concept sketch
as Razz P. Berry, a scorned man who steps

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:01.239
out of an '80s music video to exact revenge
on who he very wrongly thinks is a cheating

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:01.860
girlfriend.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:07.040
Glover also served as the musical guest under
his stage name, Childish Gambino, launching

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.660
the single that turned out to be one of the
most controversial songs of the year.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:15.799
More than 100 million people tune in to watch
the Super Bowl every year, making the halftime

00:03:15.799 --> 00:03:20.360
show one of the most criticized and scrutinized
performances of the year, if only because

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:22.060
of the sheer magnitude of the gig.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:27.180
With that many eyes, musicians need to bring
their A-Game, and when Justin Timberlake headlined

00:03:27.189 --> 00:03:31.239
the spectacular for the first time since the
infamous 2004 performance with Janet Jackson,

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:35.120
he certainly did that… assuming the A stands
for awful.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:39.439
While dressed in a camouflage outfit and a
red bandana that made him look like a golden

00:03:39.439 --> 00:03:43.650
retriever, Timberlake pushed his way through
a medley of hits like "Rock Your Body" and

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:44.360
"SexyBack."

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:49.140
He had reportedly planned to employ a Prince
hologram in his set, but canceled it after

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.810
objections from, among others, Prince himself,
who once remarked that a technological re-creation

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:58.200
of a dead musician would be "the most demonic
thing imaginable."

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:04.060
Instead, he delivered a lackluster cover of
"I Would Die 4 U" alongside an image of Minnesota's

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:05.980
finest projected onto a sheet.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:11.740
It only served as a reminder of the controversy,
not to mention inviting a comparison to Prince’s

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:15.040
own far superior halftime show in the 2007
Super Bowl.

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:20.319
Death is a major part of The Walking Dead,
and every season brings at least one hard-hitting

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.480
tragedy as fan-favorite characters struggle
against endless hordes of zombies.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:29.700
In 2018, the show gave us one of its most
excruciating, yet heartbreakingly beautiful

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:34.419
deaths when it killed off Carl Grimes, a character
who'd been with the show from the beginning,

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.200
and who represented hope for the future.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:40.860
Carl had already endured plenty of tragedies,
including the death of his mother while giving

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:45.620
birth and the violent loss of his right eye,
but remained strong, confident, and brave.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:50.120
In the end, though, Carl was bitten, and chose
to end his life in a way that revealed his

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:51.040
inner decency.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:55.680
The good, and heartbreaking news is that he
had time to put his affairs in order — bonding

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:59.810
with his little sister Judith and writing
poignant letters to his loved ones, making

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:01.360
his death even more effective.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:06.780
While it still has plenty of fans, The Walking
Dead gradually shed millions of viewers who

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.500
were tired of being manipulated by narrative
stunts.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.320
Season-ending cliffhangers, fake-out character
deaths, and killing off fan-favorite characters

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.960
in a parade of misery simply wore viewers
out.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:21.220
In late 2018, The Walking Dead once again
messed with fans.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:24.840
Earlier in the year, star Andrew Lincoln announced
that he was leaving the show.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:30.550
AMC heavily promoted the last episodes featuring
Lincoln as Rick Grimes, and most viewers assumed

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:31.120
he'd die.

00:05:31.669 --> 00:05:35.400
It certainly looked that way, when he blew
up a bridge to kill some zombies, and got

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.580
impaled on metal, only to be airlifted to
safety.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:43.020
Right after this episode aired, AMC announced
that Lincoln would go on to star in three

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:48.300
new movies about Rick, meaning that all that
fuss wasn’t for a send-off, it was just

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.880
promotion for a spin-off instead.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:54.880
It's strange and uncomfortable to consider
a world without Adventure Time.

00:05:54.889 --> 00:05:59.620
Pendleton Ward's wildly imaginative, funny,
tragic, and strange Cartoon Network series

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:03.700
changed the game for TV animation upon its
debut back in 2010.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:08.900
Over nearly 300 episodes, it created an elaborate
land called Ooo, that was slowly revealed

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:13.280
to be a post-apocalyptic world that had been
nearly destroyed by the Great Mushroom War,

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:16.460
so named for the mushroom clouds left by nuclear
weapons.

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:21.280
In the one-hour final episode, the Great Gum
War ends not with the horrific violence of

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:25.810
the Great Mushroom War (which led to the Land
of Ooo's formation), but with peace, as the

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:28.600
leaders of the two factions agree to rule
in tandem.

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:33.819
But then GOLB, the most evil thing in existence,
sees that calm as an opening to return, and

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:35.620
does all kinds of awful things.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:40.220
In the end, goodness wins out, and viewers
see BMO becoming the future ruler of Ooo.

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:45.560
BMO tells the story of the war to two young
adventurers named Shermy and Beth, who bear

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:47.320
a striking resemblance to Finn and Jake.

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:52.080
It was a thrilling, if bittersweet, finish
to a show that already ranks among modern

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:53.080
animated classics.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:59.740
Reboots were huge in 2018, including the reboot
of the 2000s pop culture sensation Queer Eye

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:00.880
for the Straight Guy.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:06.820
Both then and now, the show sends five gay-identifying
lifestyle experts to help slovenly hetero

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:09.340
dudes clean up their acts and better their
lives.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:14.870
The 2018 version turned down the camp and
turned up the emotion, as a brand new "Fab

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:19.460
Five" based in Atlanta helped individuals
improve more than just their wardrobes, making

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:24.759
connections across race, religion, and economic
status, demonstrating that healing was possible

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:25.960
in a divided culture.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:31.000
But one moment rang totally flat, reminding
viewers that this particular reality show

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:32.200
is very real.

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:37.000
On the third episode of the Queer Eye reboot,
the gang piles into a car to go help out a

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:38.340
Georgia man named Cory.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.980
Before they get there, a cop pulls them over.

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:45.960
Cast member Karamo Brown, an African-American
man, is behind the wheel, and viewers can

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:50.940
see his face fall flat as he faces a situation
with the potential to get tense at best.

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:55.909
The officer sternly approaches the vehicle
and asks Brown to get out, which Brown's co-stars

00:07:55.909 --> 00:07:56.940
firmly protest.

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:59.080
But hey, don't worry, it's all a joke!

00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:03.600
This cop is just Henry, the guy who nominated
his friend, and someone who really doesn't

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:07.800
understand that making marginalized people
feel threatened isn't all that funny.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:12.199
The only good part of it was that Karamo was
able to discuss his fear in a way that was

00:08:12.199 --> 00:08:16.720
accessible to both the audience and to Cory,
a conversation that brought a whole lot of

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:20.100
depth to a show that’s usually focused on
how to tuck in your shirt.

